  "name": "Mbyá-Guaraní prayer-house song (opy)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Sacred song and dance of the Mbyá-Guaraní of eastern Paraguay, held in evening gatherings in the opy prayer house under a religious leader; their sacred texts were recorded by León Cadogan in Ayvu Rapyta. Men accompany the singing on the mbaraka, a five-string guitar strummed in a steady pattern, and the rave, a small fiddle, while women beat the earth with takuapu bamboo stamping tubes. The guitar, fiddle and bamboo stamping-tube cards stand in for the mbaraka, rave and takuapu.",
  "recipe": "classic modern mbyá-guaraní prayer-house song, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. group vocals. classical nylon-string guitar, fiddle, tagunggak. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
